<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarbat Khalsa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sarbat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a>, &quot;the entire Khalsa&quot;, was the general assembly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> community, which met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, usually at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaisakhi">Baisakhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a>, during the 18th century. Leaders of the misls gathered there to settle disputes and plan joint campaigns, and its collective resolutions, gurmatta, were binding on all. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> stopped convening it after 1805.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarbat-khalsa.jpg" alt="Sarbat Khalsa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arorasaab · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GURUDWARA_SRI_AKAL_TAKHT_SAHIB.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A meeting of the Sarbat Khalsa opened with the Ardās, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> prayer for guidance. The assembly then chose the Panj Piare, five members who acted as its governing body: each had to be nominated, answer objections from the assembly and face a direct vote. Once elected they sat beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Harmandir Sahib</a>. Members put forward proposals, the Panj Piare settled disputes that arose, and a proposal that passed, a gurmata, was binding on all Sikhs. As a political institution the assembly served as the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-khalsa">Dal Khalsa</a> and the legislature of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-confederacy">Sikh Confederacy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first Sarbat Khalsa was called by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> before his death in 1708. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-singh-bahadur">Banda Singh Bahadur</a> in 1716 the Mughal government under Abdus Samad Khan and later his son Zakariya Khan pursued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> with a standing army, daily public executions and rewards for the heads of the killed. Persecution eased after the death of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhsiyar">Farrukhsiyar</a> in 1719, and at Vaisakhi in 1721 the assembly, through the counsel of Bhai Mani Singh, averted a clash between the Tatt Khalsa and the followers of Banda.</p><p>A Sarbat Khalsa held after the death of Bhai Tara Singh Wan in 1726 resolved to plunder government treasure in transit, raid armouries and stables, and eliminate government informers. In 1733 another accepted the government's offer of a Nawabship and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a>. On Divali, 14 October 1745, the fighting force was reorganised into 25 jathas of about 100 each, and on Baisakhi, 29 March 1748, into the 11 misls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-khalsa">Dal Khalsa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Its last meeting under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>, in 1805, discussed the pursuit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> leader Yashwantrao Holkar by British forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. The assembly was later called again at times of crisis: in 1920 by Kartar Singh Jhabbar, which named Teja Singh Bhuchhar Jathedar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akal-takht">Akal Takht</a>; on 29 April 1986 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>, which declared the rebuilding of the Akal Takht; and in 2015, against the wishes of the Shiromani Gurdwara Parbandhak Committee, which claims to be the modern form of the institution, a claim that is contested.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sarbat Khalsa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The misls were largely autonomous units that shared conquered land and met at the <b>Sarbat Khalsa</b> assemblies at Amritsar, where collective decisions, gurmatta, were taken.</blockquote><a href="/state/sikh-misls">Sikh Misls</a></li></ul></section>
